

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: what actually consumes the ID. This decides whether the design needs any backend contract at all.

  - [1mClient-side only for now[0m — the module generates and persists the ID; [38;5;153mlogin()[39m takes it as the optional third param
    and includes it in its log/return. Nothing leaves the browser until you wire up the real API. Cheapest, and matches
    the fact that [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is still a stub.
  - [1mSent in the request body[0m — the ID rides along to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Requires implementing the actual [38;5;153mfetch[39m that doesn't
    exist yet, plus deciding the payload shape with whoever owns the API.
  - [1mStored in a cookie[0m — every request carries it automatically, no call-site changes ever. Buys server-side correlation
    for free; costs cookie attributes ([38;5;153mSameSite[39m, expiry, [38;5;153mSecure[39m) and puts you squarely in consent-banner territory.
  - [1mThird-party analytics SDK[0m — Segment/PostHog/etc. own the ID. Most capability, but it's a vendor dependency and a
    much larger change.

  Cheap to add later: body and cookie, if the module owns the ID behind a small interface. Expensive: the SDK, which
  tends to want to own identity itself.

  I'd recommend [1mclient-side only[0m — with [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stubbed there's no real request to attach anything to, and keeping the
  module's surface small means sending it later is a one-line change at the call site.
